# math.h's exact functions and fenv.h, written in SemanticScript: frexp, ldexp, scalbn, scalbln, ilogb, logb,
# modf, rint, nearbyint, lrint, llrint, lround, llround, remainder, remquo, fdim, fmax, fmin, fma, nan, and nextafter,
# each in a double and a float form, and the fenv.h functions over an emulated environment.
#
# This module is generated from tools/cmathbits.dsl by tools/build.py.

cmathbits is module
cmathbits purpose "math.h's exact functions (frexp, ldexp, scalbn, ilogb, logb, modf, rint, lrint, lround, remainder, remquo, fdim, fmax, fmin, fma, nan, nextafter, double and float forms) and fenv.h, as glibc gives them, bit for bit"
cmathbits path cmathbits
cmathbits imports standard.machine
cmathbits imports standard.system
cmathbits imports standard.string
cmathbits imports standard.math
cmathbits imports standard.compare
cmathbits imports standard.convert
cmathbits imports standard.bits
cmathbits imports standard.logic
cmathbits imports standard.test
cmathbits imports kernel
cmathbits imports clibrary
cmathbits exports none

memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to read and write the memory C callers hand the library, and this module's cells of machine.state"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to ask which machine the program was compiled for (fenv.h's constants and lrint's out-of-range results differ on AArch64)"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# Every function here is exact in IEEE arithmetic: its result is the one real number the definition names, rounded once
# (or not at all), so each gives glibc's bits, signed zeros and NaN payloads included. A float form widens its
# arguments to double (exactly), computes there, and narrows the result (exactly, or by the one rounding the C
# function does: fmaf rounds to 24 bits itself, never through a double).
#
# Methods: scalbn, remainder, and remquo follow the exact integer methods of fdlibm and musl (MIT); nan reads its tag as
# glibc's strtod does; fma is the hardware's fused multiply-add. fenv.h reads and writes the hardware's own
# floating-point state (see below), and rint, lrint, and the rest obey its rounding mode. The functions raise the
# flags glibc's raise and set errno through clibrary where glibc's do (ERANGE, EDOM). Bytes 24832 to 25087 of
# machine.state belong to this module: 24848 eight bytes where a Float32's bits are moved.
scratchAt is constant
scratchAt purpose "Where a Float32's bits are moved, in machine.state"
scratchAt type Int64
scratchAt value 24848

indexOne is constant
indexOne purpose "The rounding mode index of downward"
indexOne type Int64
indexOne value 1

indexTwo is constant
indexTwo purpose "The rounding mode index of upward"
indexTwo type Int64
indexTwo value 2

okStatus is constant
okStatus purpose "A successful status"
okStatus type Int32
okStatus value 0

refusedStatus is constant
refusedStatus purpose "A refused status"
refusedStatus type Int32
refusedStatus value 1

rangeError is constant
rangeError purpose "ERANGE: a result past what its type holds"
rangeError type Int32
rangeError value 34

domainError is constant
domainError purpose "EDOM: an argument outside the function's domain"
domainError type Int32
domainError value 33

forever is constant
forever purpose "The bound of a loop that ends when its work does"
forever type Int64
forever value 9223372036854775807

